{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the full counting proof</summary>

For a finite alphabet of size $q$, the set of length-$k$ words has cardinality $q^k$. There are $m-k+1$ starting positions. If $m\ge q^k+k$, then $m-k+1\ge q^k+1$, and the window map from starting positions to block values cannot be injective. Hence two distinct positions carry equal blocks.

</details>

## Selection and subsequences

A selected list of DNA letters is itself a four-letter word. At selected length $260$, repetition is therefore forced among blocks contiguous in the selected list, including when the list is an order-preserving subsequence.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Clarify contiguous versus scattered repetition</summary>

The theorem compares adjacent blocks after one selection has formed a word. If the selection preserves genomic order, those blocks can be gapped in the original genome. This does not yet classify two independently chosen, disjoint scattered copies; that is a separate extremal problem.

</details>

## Low-complexity regions

If DNA is decoded from only $b$ effective symbols, there are at most $b^4$ encoded four-blocks. The guaranteed threshold contracts to $b^4+4$.

